dly frozen envelope membranes on intact chloroplasts and after purification. <source>Protoplasma.</source> <volume>125</volume>:<fpage>111</fpage>&#x02013;123.</citation></ref><ref id="bib10"><citation citation-type="journal">Emanuelsson, O., H. Nielsen, and G. von Heijne. <year>1999</year>. ChloroP, a neural network- based method for predicting chloroplast transit peptides and their cleavage sites. <source>Protein Sci.</source> <volume>8</volume>:<fpage>978</fpage>&#x02013;984.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10338008</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib11"><citation citation-type="journal">Ferro, M., D. Salvi, H. Riviere-Rolland, T. Vermat, D. Seigneurin-Berny, D. Grunwald, J. Garin, J. Joyard, and N. Rolland. <year>2002</year>. Integral membrane proteins of the chloroplast envelope: identification and subcellular localization of new transporters. <source>Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. USA.</source> <volume>99</volume>:<fpage>11487</fpage>&#x02013;11492.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12177442</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib12"><citation citation-type="journal">Ferro, M., D. Salvi, S. Brugiere, S. Miras, S. Kowalski, M. Louwagie, J. Garin, J. Joyard, and N. Rolland. <year>2003</year>. Proteomics of the chloroplast envelope membranes from Arabidopsis thaliana. <source>Mol. Cell. Proteomics.</source> <volume>2</volume>:<fpage>325</fpage>&#x02013;345.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12766230</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib13"><citation citation-type="journal">Frazier, A.E., R.D. Taylor, D.U. Mick, B. Warscheid, N. Stoepel, H.E. Meyer, M.T. Ryan, B. Guiard, and P. Rehling. <year>2006</year>. Mdm38 interacts with ribosomes and is a component of the mitochondrial protein export machinery. <source>J. Cell Biol.</source> <volume>172</volume>:<fpage>553</fpage>&#x02013;564.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16476776</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib14"><citation citation-type="journal">Froehlich, J.E., C.G. Wilkerson, W.K. Ray, R.S. McAndrew, K.W. Osteryoung, D.A. Gage, and B.S. Phinney. <year>2003</year>. Proteomic study of the <italic>Arabidopsis thaliana</italic> chloroplastic envelope membrane utilizing alternatives to traditional two-dimensional electrophoresis. <source>J. Proteome Res.</source> <volume>2</volume>:<fpage>413</fpage>&#x02013;425.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12938931</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib15"><citation citation-type="journal">Gerdes, L., T. Bals, E. Klostermann, M. Karl, K. Philippar, M. Hunken, J. Soll, and D. Schunemann. <year>2006</year>. A second thylakoid membrane-localized Alb3/Oxa1/YidC homologue is involved in proper chloroplast biogenesis in Arabidopsis thaliana. <source>J. Biol. Chem.</source> <volume>281</volume>:<fpage>16632</fpage>&#x02013;16642.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16595657</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib16"><citation citation-type="journal">Herrmann, J.M. <year>2003</year>. Converting bacteria to organelles: evolution of mitochondrial protein sorting. <source>Trends Microbiol.</source> <volume>11</volume>:<fpage>74</fpage>&#x02013;79.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12598129</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib17"><citation citation-type="journal">Herrmann, J.M., and W. Neupert. <year>2003</year>. Protein insertion into the inner membrane of mitochondria. <source>IUBMB Life.</source> <volume>55</volume>:<fpage>219</fpage>&#x02013;225.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12880202</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib18"><citation citation-type="journal">Herrmann, J.M., and N. Bonnefoy. <year>2004</year>. Protein export across the inner membrane of mitochondria: the nature of translocated domains determines the dependence on the Oxa1 translocase. <source>J. Biol. Chem.</source> <volume>279</volume>:<fpage>2507</fpage>&#x02013;2512.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14593095</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib19"><citation citation-type="journal">Herrmann, J.M., W. Neupert, and R.A. Stuart. <year>1997</year>. Insertion into the mitochondrial inner membrane of a polytopic protein, the nuclear-encoded Oxa1p. <source>EMBO J.</source> <volume>16</volume>:<fpage>2217</fpage>&#x02013;2226.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9171337</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib20"><citation citation-type="journal">Inaba, T., M. Alvarez-Huerta, M. Li, J. Bauer, C. Ewers, F. Kessler, and D.J. Schnell. <year>2005</year>. Arabidopsis tic110 is essential for the assembly and function of the protein import machinery of plastids. <source>Plant Cell.</source> <volume>17</volume>:<fpage>1482</fpage>&#x02013;1496.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15829604</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib21"><citation citation-type="journal">Inoue, K., A.J. Baldwin, R.L. Shipman, K. Matsui, S.M. Theg, and M. Ohme-Takagi. <year>2005</year>. Complete maturation of the plastid protein translocation channel requires a type I signal peptidase. <source>J. Cell Biol.</source> <volume>171</volume>:<fpage>425</fpage>&#x02013;430.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16275749</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib22"><citation citation-type="journal">Jackson, D.T., J.E. Froehlich, and K. Keegstra. <year>1998</year>. The hydrophilic domain of Tic110, an inner envelope membrane component of the chloroplastic protein translocation apparatus, faces the stromal compartment. <source>J. Biol. Chem.</source> <volume>273</volume>:<fpage>16583</fpage>&#x02013;16588.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9632730</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib23"><citation citation-type="journal">Jarvis, P. <year>2004</year>. Organellar proteomics: chloroplasts in the spotlight. <source>Curr. Biol.</source> <volume>14</volume>:<fpage>R317</fpage>&#x02013;R319.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15084303</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib24"><citation citation-type="journal">Jarvis, P., and C. Robinson. <year>2004</year>. Mechanisms of protein import and routing in chloroplasts. <source>Curr. Biol.</source> <volume>14</volume>:<fpage>R1064</fpage>&#x02013;R1077.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15620643</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib25"><citation citation-type="journal">Joyard, J., E. Teyssier, C. Miege, D. Berny-Seigneurin, E. Marechal, M.A. Block, A.J. Dorne, N. Rolland, G. Ajlani, and R. Douce. <year>1998</year>. The biochemical machinery of plastid envelope membranes. <source>Plant Physiol.</source> <volume>118</volume>:<fpage>715</fpage>&#x02013;723.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9808715</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib26"><citation citation-type="journal">Keegstra, K., and A.E. Yousif. <year>1986</year>. Isolation and characterization of chloroplast envelope membranes. <source>Methods Enzymol.</source> <volume>118</volume>:<fpage>316</fpage>&#x02013;325.</citation></ref><ref id="bib27"><citation citation-type="journal">Keegstra, K., and K. Cline. <year>1999</year>. Protein import and routing systems of chloroplasts. <source>Plant Cell.</source> <volume>11</volume>:<fpage>557</fpage>&#x02013;570.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10213778</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib28"><citation citation-type="journal">Kessler, F., and G. Blobel. <year>1996</year>. Interaction of the protein import and folding machineries in the chloroplast. <source>Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. USA.</source> <volume>93</volume>:<fpage>7684</fpage>&#x02013;7689.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8755536</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib29"><citation citation-type="journal">Kessler, F., and D.J. Schnell. <year>2006</year>. The function and diversity of plastid protein import pathways: a multilane GTPase highway into plastids. <source>Traffic.</source> <volume>7</volume>:<fpage>248</fpage>&#x02013;257.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16497220</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib30"><citation citation-type="journal">Knight, J.S., and J.C. Gray. <year>1995</year>. The N-terminal hydrophobic region of the mature phosphate translocator is sufficient for targeting to the chloroplast inner envelope membrane. <source>Plant Cell.</source> <volume>7</volume>:<fpage>1421</fpage>&#x02013;1432.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8589626</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib31"><citation citation-type="journal">Kou